cs are a key part to understanding  problems and what's happening with your  system  traces and logs have the context and  details to help you diagnose the cause  of said issues but they're not always  linked together  so going from a metric to the requests  and logs that generated that particular  metric data point  is difficult and important to be able to  diagnose issues quickly  what's the solution a feature we call  example traces  otherwise known as trace exemplars in  the screenshot on the left you can see a  heat map distribution of latency  that has two important additions one  there are aggregate percentile lines  that help us understand  how this distribution is trending second  there are example distributed traces  that have been sampled  these represent complete requests and  you can actually drill into that request  without leaving the context of your  monitoring tool  which you'll see in a moment  now let's see this in action the demo  we're about to show you  is of our example application called  online boutique  in this scenario users of the  application who normally experience  great performance have started to notice  extremely degraded latency  the developer in the video will show you  how to use the google cloud operations  tool suite  and example traces as i mentioned  earlier to quickly diagnose said issue  here we will show how we can use cloud  monitoring  and cloud trace to quickly understand  where we should be focusing our  attention  with regards to a particular issue  in this case we have an alert related to  99th percentile latency  which has spiked quite significantly  recently  drilling into our performance dashboard  we can see that there was a significant  increase in latency around the time of  the alert  this seems to possibly correlate  with error rate but not exactly  this increase can be shown both by the  distribution itself but also the  aggregate percentile lines and how they  have increased over time  let's drill in further to the inflection  point  now typically this is actually quite  difficult to go from here  understanding the specific requests that  led to that metric data point  is not always easily available however  with google cloud operations we can  overlay example traces  onto this metrics graph now we can  easily without switching context drill  into exactly what was happening  at the time the issue was observed  so in this instance this is a call to  the product service  which is making a call to get product  and making further calls to get  product which is an interesting behavior  out of the seven seconds duration about  6.9 of them were related to get product  let's see if this seems to correlate  with other types of requests  this is another similar such product  call which has a similar profile of  child calls to the product catalog  service  this here is a cart operation so that's  different  however when we look further it has the  same  get repeated get product call in the  product catalog  service that we saw earlier  and if we look further we're going to  see similar behavior  of product catalog service operations  representing the majority of the latency  now to be sure of this let's compare  with traces prior to the issue here's a  similar get product call  and it's making calls the recommendation  service  again to the get product service  it makes a few calls to get product  which is what we saw  before however if you look at the total  latency  only 31 milliseconds were spent within  the get product service this compares to  20 seconds and some of the other  examples we were  investigating  so it definitely seems to be a delta in  the length of time of get  product calls that is representative of  this latency  at this point i have a viable hypothesis  for what caused this issue  and i got there without actually leaving  my monitoring dashboard  from here i would look at logging  perhaps debugging  error reporting to try and understand  what was actually 