ing what we can  see here  is after calling list recommendations  the system is making multiple calls  to get product from the product catalog  service that's interesting  in fact in this 19 second trace which  started as a get cart request almost  all of the latency is spent within those  get product calls  that's definitely not what we want to  see and it suggests that that's where  the issue  is occurring  we're going to use cloud logging to  understand what's happening within that  product catalog  service again it's very easy to find  this out by drilling in  to the resource type in the field  explorer  we're going to filter for the pod named  product catalog service  now what we can see is roughly at the  time of the error there is a  massive spike in logs from this service  narrowing down to this time interval we  can see that almost all of these logs  are successfully parsed product catalog  at this point it's safe to assume that  this is where i should focus my  attention  i'm going to open up my ide and  understand what's happening  with the parsing of the product catalog  to find out why is it actually  successfully parse the product catalog  so often  this is a good example of how to use the  operations toolset  including logging trace monitoring  and more to quickly narrow down a hard  to find problem  sometimes a problem can't be easily  found in the logs  or the traces it has to do more with the  functioning of your running code  or perhaps it's about cost optimization  this is where profiler  is extremely useful kellyanne is going  to show you  how you can use profile to solve these  needle in the haystack type problems  cloud profiler can help developers  understand how their services  are spending compute and memory  resources we will explore  how profiler can be used to identify  optimization opportunities  and help you run your services more  efficiently  profiler supports four languages java  go node.js and python for this  walkthrough  we will 