 developers to understand how their  services  are spending resources and where they  should start  their optimization efforts  our final scenario talks about how to  debug code  when it counts if you've ever had a  scenario  where you could not reproduce an issue  in your development environment  then this is for you if you've ever had  a scenario where that one line of  logging  that you needed was not there when you  needed it  this is for you in this next demo  we're going to see how cloud debugger  can help you debug in production  when you need it most  to start this journey we're going to  open error reporting  error reporting automatically surfaces  errors found in the logs for easy  discoverability  within the error group we can see the  trend where this error has occurred  where in the code this error has  occurred and easily linked to the logs  that generated the error  exploring these logs we can see quite  easily  that this is exclusively caused by the  currency service  we can inspect the logs for further  details  including resource context  however this doesn't actually show us  what's happening and causing this  particular problem  for that we're going to open cloud  debugger  debugger allows us to see what is  happening in our actual running  production code  in the logs you saw that the error was  happening at line 55  of the currency service server js  clicking here we can take a snapshot of  what's happening at the code at that  exact position  inspecting the details of the variables  this was done without restarting the  service  without pausing the service completely  safe in production  now the cause isn't obvious here but we  can see from the code that if  results.units is not greater than zero  it would throw this error so let's see  what results.units  is set at in this case  it's set at not a number so that's  obviously not going to work let's see  how this was actually set  and in this case where we set that value  was also  not a valid 